// String operations, in Prismio.
//
// **Three representation operations are irreducible. Everything else here is
// Prismio.**
//
//     __builtin_string_len       read the carried byte count
//     __builtin_string_byte_at   read one already-bounded byte
//     __builtin_string_put_byte  write one already-bounded byte
//
// These are not functions and have no declarations. Sema owns their signatures,
// AIF owns their non-retaining effects, and codegen lowers them directly to an
// `extractvalue`, GEP/load, or GEP/store. Their reserved names are only the
// call-shaped spelling by which the standard library asks the compiler for an
// operation. Older C string routines used by compiler-internal code remain
// ordinary foreign symbols and are no longer confused with these builtins.
//
// `str_with_capacity` is the one real runtime call: memory allocation remains a
// runtime capability, and the frontend builds its String pair from the requested
// length.
//
// Equality, comparison, checked character access, concatenation, slicing and
// Char conversion are all ordinary Prismio below. Importing this module neither
// declares nor calls their older C implementations. The first five live in the
// `impl String` block rather than as prefixed free functions, because they are
// what the operators lower to -- see the note over them.
//
// What every wrapper adds is the **ownership contract**, declared once. An
// `extern fn` with no contract has unknown provenance, so the analysis widens it
// to Shared, the result gets no owner, and it leaks. That used to be every
// application's problem, once per application.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The three rules that shape this file
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **1. Never accumulate into a reassigned binding.**
//
//     let mut out = ""
//     while (...) { out = out.concat(piece) }         // leaks, every iteration
//
// An assignment releases nothing -- src/ir/stmt.psm marks a reassigned binding
// as never droppable, because leaking beats double-freeing -- so every
// intermediate is unreachable and unfreed. This is why every builder here
// allocates its result **once** with `str_with_capacity` and fills it, rather
// than growing a string.
//
// Before `str_with_capacity` existed the only way to obey this rule was
// recursion, one frame per character. That cost n allocations, copied O(n^2)
// bytes, and put a stack ceiling on the whole family at 150 000 characters.
// One allocation the caller writes into removed all three: 5 000 000 characters
// now works, and a 13-call program that allocated 58 times allocates 29.
//
// **2. Every `return` is this function's own allocation.**
//
// `return strToLower(s)` from another function is a *pass-through*: the buffer
// was allocated a frame down, `aif_owns_call_result_at_node` declines it because
// from the call site a value made below is indistinguishable from one handed
// down, and the caller gets no drop. Measured -- `strToUpper` delegating to a
// shared body read 1 allocated / 0 released; inlined, 1/1. It is why the two
// case-mapping loops are written out twice.
//
// A bare `return ""` fails for a different reason: static storage contributes no
// allocation site at all, so one such return makes the whole function's result
// unowned. `strEmpty()` exists so the empty case is an allocation like any other.
//
// **3. Prefer bounded byte loops.**
//
// Recomputing length with `strlen` is O(n), so a loop with it in the condition
// becomes quadratic: measured on 200 scans of 40 000 characters, 15.08s with it
// in the loop against 0.48s hoisted. `__builtin_string_len` is a field read, and
// the hoisting in this file is left in place because it is still clearer, not
// because it still costs anything.
//
// The byte builtins do **no upper-bound check** -- that is what makes them O(1)
// -- so they are used only where the bound is already established, which in
// this file means "inside a loop bounded by a hoisted length".
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// **Callers must bind what they get.** `println(strTrim(s))` leaks and
// `let t = strTrim(s)  println(t)` does not -- an owned result passed straight
// into a parameter is a value nothing names, and nothing names it is nothing
// frees it. Measured: 70 allocated / 57 released unbound, 58 / 58 bound. That is
// the language's rule rather than this module's, but this is where most callers
// will meet it.
//
// See RUNTIME.md for which C symbols remain and why.

import std.option

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Primitives
//
// The representation operations above are compiler builtins and deliberately
// have no declarations here. Only allocation crosses the runtime ABI.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Allocation plus the write builtin make building a string a loop instead of a
// recursion. See the note over `str_with_capacity` in runtime/lang_runtime.c:
// without them an
// n-byte result costs n allocations, copies O(n^2) bytes, and has to recurse
// because the loop form leaks -- which capped these functions at 150 000
// characters. The write builtin does not check its upper bound; every use below
// is immediately after a `str_with_capacity` whose length bounds the loop.
internal extern fn str_with_capacity(length: Int) -> String produce (free )

// Optional search accelerators, not part of the irreducible String core.
// `str_find_byte` delegates a one-byte scan to libc. `str_find_byte_pair` is a
// bounded 128-bit NEON/SSE2 candidate scan with a scalar fallback, which the
// long-needle prefilter below drives. `str_find_needle` is the whole search for
// a needle of 2..32 bytes -- pair selection, the scan and verification -- because
// doing those here cost a runtime crossing per candidate. Two-Way factorisation
// and the long-needle policy stay in Prismio.
private extern fn str_find_byte(s: String borrow , from: Int, b: Char) -> Int
private extern fn str_find_byte_pair(s: String borrow , from: Int, last: Int,
offset1: Int, byte1: Char,
offset2: Int, byte2: Char) -> Int
private extern fn str_find_needle(s: String bytes , from: Int, length: Int,
needle: String bytes , needleLength: Int) -> Int

// Byte length, not character count. `Char` is a byte here and there is no
// Unicode scalar type yet, so a multi-byte UTF-8 character counts as its bytes.
internal fn strLength(s: String) -> Int {
    return __builtin_string_len(s)
}

internal fn strByteAt(s: String, index: Int) -> Int {
    return __builtin_string_byte_at(s, index) as U8 as Int
}

// Copy `count` bytes between already-bounded ranges. Allocation deliberately
// stays in each public producer: under the current ownership analysis a native
// function that merely returns another function's allocation is a pass-through.
private fn strCopyRangeInto(out: String, outStart: Int, source: String, sourceStart: Int, count: Int) {
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < count) {
        __builtin_string_put_byte(out, outStart + i, __builtin_string_byte_at(source, sourceStart + i))
        i = i + 1
    }
}

// `length` bytes from `start`. A range reaching past the end is clamped rather
// than refused.
internal fn strSubstring(s: String, start: Int, length: Int) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut first = start
    let mut count = length
    if (first < 0 or first >= slen) {
        first = slen
        count = 0
    }
    if (count < 0) { count = 0 }
    if (first + count > slen) { count = slen - first }
    // Twelve bytes or fewer and the result *is* the pair: no allocation, nothing
    // to release, and the copy is the twelve bytes themselves. This is the German
    // short form (see the layout note in runtime/llvm-api-backend.c), and this
    // test is the caller's half of the builtin's contract -- it does not bound
    // `count` itself, for the same reason `__builtin_string_byte_at` does not
    // bound its index.
    if (count <= 12) { return __builtin_string_inline(s, first, count) }
    // Longer than the pair can hold, so it becomes a **view**: a pointer into `s`
    // and a length, with no allocation and no copy at all. `count > 12` is what
    // makes this sound -- a source with that many bytes to give cannot itself be
    // the inline form, so it has a pointer to offset.
    return __builtin_string_view(s, first, count)
}

internal fn strFromChar(c: Char) -> String {
    // Prismio Strings cannot contain NUL, so a NUL Char maps to empty text.
    if (c == '\0') { return str_with_capacity(0) }
    let out = str_with_capacity(1)
    __builtin_string_put_byte(out, 0, c)
    return out
}

// An owned empty string.
//
// Not a convenience. `return ""` in a String-returning function returns static
// storage, and one such return makes every result of that function unowned --
// so the callers that *did* allocate leak too. This is how the empty case stays
// an allocation like any other.
internal fn strEmpty() -> String {
    return str_with_capacity(0)
}

// A fresh copy the caller owns.
//
// Twelve bytes or fewer are copied into the pair itself, as `concat` and `slice`
// do: no allocation, and two short Strings holding the same text are then
// bit-identical, which is what `==`'s register fast path needs. A
// `Map<String, V>` stores its keys through this (`copyOf`), and a heap copy of a
// short key made every successful probe a `memcmp` against the inline key it was
// looked up with -- 47,992 of them in `word_frequency`.
internal fn strClone(s: String) -> String {
    let n = __builtin_string_len(s)
    if (n <= 12) { return __builtin_string_inline(s, 0, n) }
    let out = str_with_capacity(n)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, s, 0, n)
    return out
}

internal fn strIsEmpty(s: String) -> Bool {
    return __builtin_string_len(s) == 0
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Character classification
//
// Byte predicates, ASCII only. Named so that the call sites below read as what
// they test rather than as arithmetic on character codes.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

internal fn charIsDigit(c: Char) -> Bool {
    return c >= '0' and c <= '9'
}

internal fn charIsLower(c: Char) -> Bool {
    return c >= 'a' and c <= 'z'
}

internal fn charIsUpper(c: Char) -> Bool {
    return c >= 'A' and c <= 'Z'
}

internal fn charIsAlpha(c: Char) -> Bool {
    return charIsLower(c) or charIsUpper(c)
}

internal fn charIsAlnum(c: Char) -> Bool {
    return charIsAlpha(c) or charIsDigit(c)
}

// Space, tab, newline, carriage return. The same four bytes the C `str_trim`
// recognised, kept identical so that porting it changed no behaviour.
internal fn charIsSpace(c: Char) -> Bool {
    if (c == ' ') { return true }
    if (c == '\t') { return true }
    if (c == '\n') { return true }
    return c == '\r'
}

internal fn charToUpper(c: Char) -> Char {
    if (charIsLower(c) == false) { return c }
    return ((c as U8) - 32) as Char
}

internal fn charToLower(c: Char) -> Char {
    if (charIsUpper(c) == false) { return c }
    return ((c as U8) + 32) as Char
}

internal fn charDigitValue(c: Char) -> Int {
    return (c as U8 as Int) - 48
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Searching
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Whether `s` matches `needle` starting at `at`. Every search below is written
// in terms of this one, so the bounds reasoning lives in a single place.
private fn strMatchesAtIn(s: String, needle: String, at: Int, slen: Int, nlen: Int) -> Bool {
    if (at < 0) { return false }
    if (at + nlen > slen) { return false }
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < nlen) {
        // The guard above licenses the unchecked byte builtin: it does no upper
        // bound check and is therefore only safe where the bound is already
        // established. It is here -- `at + nlen <= slen`.
        if (__builtin_string_byte_at(s, at + i) != __builtin_string_byte_at(needle, i)) { return false }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return true
}

// Lengths are passed into the inner helper so its byte loop has one established
// bound. The length builtin is an O(1) read from the String pair. Before fat Strings,
// leaving length calls in loop conditions cost 15.08s against the C
// `str_index_of`'s 0.50s on 200 scans of 40 000 characters; hoisting them brought
// it to 0.65s. Keeping the bound explicit still documents why unchecked
// the unchecked byte read is safe here.
internal fn strMatchesAt(s: String, needle: String, at: Int) -> Bool {
    return strMatchesAtIn(s, needle, at, __builtin_string_len(s), __builtin_string_len(needle))
}

internal fn strStartsWith(s: String, prefix: String) -> Bool {
    return strMatchesAt(s, prefix, 0)
}

internal fn strEndsWith(s: String, suffix: String) -> Bool {
    return strMatchesAt(s, suffix, __builtin_string_len(s) - __builtin_string_len(suffix))
}

// A lower rank means a byte is expected to be rarer in ordinary text. These
// lowercase ranks are the default distribution used by memchr::memmem's packed
// pair search; uppercase shares its lowercase rank because Prismio has one
// search policy rather than separate corpora. Exact values matter less than the
// ordering: the two least common needle bytes make the strongest SIMD filter.
private fn strSearchByteRank(c: Char) -> Int {
    let mut b = c
    if (charIsUpper(b)) { b =((b as U8) + 32) as Char }
    if (b == 'a') { return 249 }
    if (b == 'b') { return 216 }
    if (b == 'c') { return 238 }
    if (b == 'd') { return 236 }
    if (b == 'e') { return 253 }
    if (b == 'f') { return 227 }
    if (b == 'g') { return 218 }
    if (b == 'h') { return 230 }
    if (b == 'i') { return 247 }
    if (b == 'j') { return 135 }
    if (b == 'k') { return 180 }
    if (b == 'l') { return 241 }
    if (b == 'm') { return 233 }
    if (b == 'n') { return 246 }
    if (b == 'o') { return 244 }
    if (b == 'p') { return 231 }
    if (b == 'q') { return 139 }
    if (b == 'r') { return 245 }
    if (b == 's') { return 243 }
    if (b == 't') { return 251 }
    if (b == 'u') { return 235 }
    if (b == 'v') { return 201 }
    if (b == 'w') { return 196 }
    if (b == 'x') { return 240 }
    if (b == 'y') { return 214 }
    if (b == 'z') { return 152 }
    if (b == ' ') { return 255 }
    if (charIsDigit(b)) { return 220 }
    if ((b as U8 as Int) >= 128) { return 254 }
    return 100
}

// Compute one of the two maximal suffixes used to obtain a critical
// factorisation for Two-Way search. The high 32 bits of the result hold the
// factorisation position and the low 32 bits hold its period. Packing the pair
// keeps this helper allocation-free until Prismio has stack-only tuple returns.
private fn strMaximalSuffix(needle: String, nlen: Int, orderGreater: Bool) -> I64 {
    let mut left = 0
    let mut right = 1
    let mut offset = 0
    let mut period = 1
    while (right + offset < nlen) {
        let a = __builtin_string_byte_at(needle, right + offset)
        let b = __builtin_string_byte_at(needle, left + offset)
        if ((a < b and orderGreater == false) or
        (a > b and orderGreater)) {
            right = right + offset + 1
            offset = 0
            period = right - left
        } else if (a == b) {
            if (offset + 1 == period) {
                right = right + offset + 1
                offset = 0
            } else {
                offset = offset + 1
            }
        } else {
            left = right
            right = right + 1
            offset = 0
            period = 1
        }
    }
    return ((left as I64) << 32) |(period as I64)
}

// Crochemore-Perrin Two-Way search. This is the linear-time core used by
// Rust's standard `str::find`: split the needle at a critical factorisation,
// compare the right half forwards and the left half backwards, then shift by a
// period that cannot hide a match. A 64-bit byteset cheaply rejects tails that
// occur nowhere in the relevant part of the needle.
private fn strPrefilteredTwoWayIndexOfFrom(s: String, needle: String, from: Int,
slen: Int, nlen: Int,
pairIndex1: Int, pairByte1: Char,
pairIndex2: Int, pairByte2: Char) -> Int {
    let suffixLess = strMaximalSuffix(needle, nlen, false)
    let suffixGreater = strMaximalSuffix(needle, nlen, true)
    let lessPosition =(suffixLess >> 32) as Int
    let greaterPosition =(suffixGreater >> 32) as Int
    let mut critical = greaterPosition
    let mut period = suffixGreater as Int
    if (lessPosition > greaterPosition) {
        critical = lessPosition
        period = suffixLess as Int
    }

    // If the critical prefix repeats at `period`, it is the exact short period
    // and matched bytes can be remembered across shifts. Otherwise the period
    // is long; use the safe lower-bound shift and no memory.
    let mut exactPeriod = period + critical <= nlen
    let mut prefixAt = 0
    while (exactPeriod and prefixAt < critical) {
        if (__builtin_string_byte_at(needle, prefixAt) !=
        __builtin_string_byte_at(needle, period + prefixAt)) {
            exactPeriod = false
        }
        prefixAt = prefixAt + 1
    }
    if (exactPeriod == false) {
        let rightLength = nlen - critical
        if (critical > rightLength) { period = critical + 1 }
        else { period = rightLength + 1 }
    }

    let mut bytesetEnd = nlen
    if (exactPeriod) { bytesetEnd = period }
    let mut byteset: U64 = 0 as U64
    let mut setAt = 0
    while (setAt < bytesetEnd) {
        let bit =(__builtin_string_byte_at(needle, setAt) as U8 as Int) & 63
        byteset = byteset |((1 as U64) <<(bit as U64))
        setAt = setAt + 1
    }

    let mut position = from
    let lastStart = slen - nlen
    let mut prefilterActive = true
    let mut prefilterSkips = 0
    let mut prefilterDistance = 0
    if (exactPeriod) {
        // Short-period CP1 keeps a memory boundary across shifts. It has its
        // own loop so the hot path contains no repeated period-kind branches.
        let mut memory = 0
        while (position + nlen <= slen) {
            if (prefilterActive) {
                let before = position
                let candidate = str_find_byte_pair(s, before, lastStart,
                pairIndex1, pairByte1,
                pairIndex2, pairByte2)
                if (candidate < 0) { return - 1 }
                position = candidate
                memory = 0
                prefilterSkips = prefilterSkips + 1
                prefilterDistance = prefilterDistance + candidate - before
                if (prefilterSkips >= 50) {
                    if (prefilterDistance < prefilterSkips * 8) {
                        prefilterActive = false
                    }
                    prefilterSkips = 0
                    prefilterDistance = 0
                }
            }
            let tailBit =(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, position + nlen - 1) as U8 as Int) & 63
            if (((byteset >>(tailBit as U64)) &(1 as U64)) ==(0 as U64)) {
                position = position + nlen
                memory = 0
            } else {
                let mut rightAt = critical
                if (memory > rightAt) { rightAt = memory }
                while (rightAt < nlen and
                __builtin_string_byte_at(needle, rightAt) == __builtin_string_byte_at(s, position + rightAt)) {
                    rightAt = rightAt + 1
                }
                if (rightAt < nlen) {
                    position = position + rightAt - critical + 1
                    memory = 0
                } else {
                    let mut leftAt = critical
                    let mut leftMismatch = false
                    while (leftAt > memory and leftMismatch == false) {
                        leftAt = leftAt - 1
                        if (__builtin_string_byte_at(needle, leftAt) !=
                        __builtin_string_byte_at(s, position + leftAt)) {
                            leftMismatch = true
                        }
                    }
                    if (leftMismatch == false) { return position }
                    position = position + period
                    memory = nlen - period
                }
            }
        }
        return - 1
    }

    // Long-period CP2 never memorises a matched prefix. Keeping this separate
    // removes five data-dependent exact-period checks from every iteration.
    while (position + nlen <= slen) {
        if (prefilterActive) {
            let before = position
            let candidate = str_find_byte_pair(s, before, lastStart,
            pairIndex1, pairByte1,
            pairIndex2, pairByte2)
            if (candidate < 0) { return - 1 }
            position = candidate
            prefilterSkips = prefilterSkips + 1
            prefilterDistance = prefilterDistance + candidate - before
            if (prefilterSkips >= 50) {
                if (prefilterDistance < prefilterSkips * 8) {
                    prefilterActive = false
                }
                prefilterSkips = 0
                prefilterDistance = 0
            }
        }
        let tailBit =(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, position + nlen - 1) as U8 as Int) & 63
        if (((byteset >>(tailBit as U64)) &(1 as U64)) ==(0 as U64)) {
            position = position + nlen
        } else {
            let mut rightAt = critical
            while (rightAt < nlen and
            __builtin_string_byte_at(needle, rightAt) == __builtin_string_byte_at(s, position + rightAt)) {
                rightAt = rightAt + 1
            }
            if (rightAt < nlen) {
                position = position + rightAt - critical + 1
            } else {
                let mut leftAt = critical
                let mut leftMismatch = false
                while (leftAt > 0 and leftMismatch == false) {
                    leftAt = leftAt - 1
                    if (__builtin_string_byte_at(needle, leftAt) !=
                    __builtin_string_byte_at(s, position + leftAt)) {
                        leftMismatch = true
                    }
                }
                if (leftMismatch == false) { return position }
                position = position + period
            }
        }
    }
    return - 1
}

// The unprefiltered specialization used after a short needle produces dense
// candidates. Prismio does not yet specialize a Bool argument across a call,
// and leaving the prefilter disabled at run time doubled this pathological
// case. Keeping the compact Two-Way loop separate restores Rust-std parity and
// also avoids carrying four unused pair arguments through the hot function.
private fn strTwoWayIndexOfFrom(s: String, needle: String, from: Int,
slen: Int, nlen: Int) -> Int {
    let suffixLess = strMaximalSuffix(needle, nlen, false)
    let suffixGreater = strMaximalSuffix(needle, nlen, true)
    let lessPosition =(suffixLess >> 32) as Int
    let greaterPosition =(suffixGreater >> 32) as Int
    let mut critical = greaterPosition
    let mut period = suffixGreater as Int
    if (lessPosition > greaterPosition) {
        critical = lessPosition
        period = suffixLess as Int
    }

    let mut exactPeriod = period + critical <= nlen
    let mut prefixAt = 0
    while (exactPeriod and prefixAt < critical) {
        if (__builtin_string_byte_at(needle, prefixAt) !=
        __builtin_string_byte_at(needle, period + prefixAt)) {
            exactPeriod = false
        }
        prefixAt = prefixAt + 1
    }
    if (exactPeriod == false) {
        let rightLength = nlen - critical
        if (critical > rightLength) { period = critical + 1 }
        else { period = rightLength + 1 }
    }

    let mut bytesetEnd = nlen
    if (exactPeriod) { bytesetEnd = period }
    let mut byteset: U64 = 0 as U64
    let mut setAt = 0
    while (setAt < bytesetEnd) {
        let bit =(__builtin_string_byte_at(needle, setAt) as U8 as Int) & 63
        byteset = byteset |((1 as U64) <<(bit as U64))
        setAt = setAt + 1
    }

    let mut position = from
    if (exactPeriod) {
        let mut memory = 0
        while (position + nlen <= slen) {
            let tailBit =(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, position + nlen - 1) as U8 as Int) & 63
            if (((byteset >>(tailBit as U64)) &(1 as U64)) ==(0 as U64)) {
                position = position + nlen
                memory = 0
            } else {
                let mut rightAt = critical
                if (memory > rightAt) { rightAt = memory }
                while (rightAt < nlen and
                __builtin_string_byte_at(needle, rightAt) == __builtin_string_byte_at(s, position + rightAt)) {
                    rightAt = rightAt + 1
                }
                if (rightAt < nlen) {
                    position = position + rightAt - critical + 1
                    memory = 0
                } else {
                    let mut leftAt = critical
                    let mut leftMismatch = false
                    while (leftAt > memory and leftMismatch == false) {
                        leftAt = leftAt - 1
                        if (__builtin_string_byte_at(needle, leftAt) !=
                        __builtin_string_byte_at(s, position + leftAt)) {
                            leftMismatch = true
                        }
                    }
                    if (leftMismatch == false) { return position }
                    position = position + period
                    memory = nlen - period
                }
            }
        }
        return - 1
    }

    while (position + nlen <= slen) {
        let tailBit =(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, position + nlen - 1) as U8 as Int) & 63
        if (((byteset >>(tailBit as U64)) &(1 as U64)) ==(0 as U64)) {
            position = position + nlen
        } else {
            let mut rightAt = critical
            while (rightAt < nlen and
            __builtin_string_byte_at(needle, rightAt) == __builtin_string_byte_at(s, position + rightAt)) {
                rightAt = rightAt + 1
            }
            if (rightAt < nlen) {
                position = position + rightAt - critical + 1
            } else {
                let mut leftAt = critical
                let mut leftMismatch = false
                while (leftAt > 0 and leftMismatch == false) {
                    leftAt = leftAt - 1
                    if (__builtin_string_byte_at(needle, leftAt) !=
                    __builtin_string_byte_at(s, position + leftAt)) {
                        leftMismatch = true
                    }
                }
                if (leftMismatch == false) { return position }
                position = position + period
            }
        }
    }
    return - 1
}

// The first index at or after `from` where `needle` occurs, or -1.
//
// The empty needle matches at `from`, which is what makes `strReplaceAll` with
// an empty pattern terminate instead of looping forever.
// One-byte needles use libc's vectorised byte search, and needles up to 32 bytes
// the runtime's packed-pair search (`str_find_needle`). Native Two-Way is the
// linear fallback for both dense short-needle inputs and the long-needle core.
internal fn strIndexOfFrom(s: String, needle: String, from: Int) -> Int {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let nlen = __builtin_string_len(needle)
    let mut i = from
    if (i < 0) { i = 0 }
    if (nlen == 0) {
        if (i <= slen) { return i }
        return - 1
    }
    if (i + nlen > slen) { return - 1 }
    if (nlen == 1) {
        let hit = str_find_byte(s, i, __builtin_string_byte_at(needle, 0))
        if (hit <= slen - 1) { return hit }
        return - 1
    }
    if (nlen <= 32) {
        let found = str_find_needle(s, i, slen, needle, nlen)
        if (found >= - 1) { return found }
        return strTwoWayIndexOfFrom(s, needle, - 2 - found, slen, nlen)
    }

    // Longer needles select their two least-common bytes to prefilter Two-Way,
    // and the prefilter disables itself if it stops skipping useful distance.
    let mut index1 = 0
    let mut index2 = 1
    let mut byte1 = __builtin_string_byte_at(needle, index1)
    let mut byte2 = __builtin_string_byte_at(needle, index2)
    let mut rank1 = strSearchByteRank(byte1)
    let mut rank2 = strSearchByteRank(byte2)
    if (rank2 < rank1) {
        let oldIndex = index1
        let oldByte = byte1
        let oldRank = rank1
        index1 = index2
        byte1 = byte2
        rank1 = rank2
        index2 = oldIndex
        byte2 = oldByte
        rank2 = oldRank
    }

    let mut p = 2
    while (p < nlen) {
        let b = __builtin_string_byte_at(needle, p)
        let rank = strSearchByteRank(b)
        if (rank < rank1) {
            index2 = index1
            byte2 = byte1
            rank2 = rank1
            index1 = p
            byte1 = b
            rank1 = rank
        } else if (b != byte1 and rank < rank2) {
            index2 = p
            byte2 = b
            rank2 = rank
        }
        p = p + 1
    }

    // Large needles use the same pair scan as a prefilter around Two-Way. Its
    // 50-skip/8-byte effectiveness window makes dense inputs fall back to pure
    // Two-Way while selective inputs retain memory-bandwidth SIMD scanning.
    return strPrefilteredTwoWayIndexOfFrom(s, needle, i, slen, nlen,
    index1, byte1, index2, byte2)
}

internal fn strIndexOf(s: String, needle: String) -> Int {
    return strIndexOfFrom(s, needle, 0)
}

internal fn strLastIndexOf(s: String, needle: String) -> Int {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let nlen = __builtin_string_len(needle)
    let mut i = slen - nlen
    while (i >= 0) {
        if (strMatchesAtIn(s, needle, i, slen, nlen)) { return i }
        i = i - 1
    }
    return - 1
}

internal fn strContains(s: String, needle: String) -> Bool {
    return strIndexOf(s, needle) >= 0
}

internal fn strCountOf(s: String, needle: String) -> Int {
    if (__builtin_string_len(needle) == 0) { return 0 }
    let mut n = 0
    let mut at = strIndexOf(s, needle)
    while (at >= 0) {
        n = n + 1
        at = strIndexOfFrom(s, needle, at + __builtin_string_len(needle))
    }
    return n
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Trimming
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

internal fn strTrimStart(s: String) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen and charIsSpace(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, i))) {
        i = i + 1
    }
    let count = slen - i
    let out = str_with_capacity(count)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, s, i, count)
    return out
}

internal fn strTrimEnd(s: String) -> String {
    let mut n = __builtin_string_len(s)
    while (n > 0 and charIsSpace(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, n - 1))) {
        n = n - 1
    }
    let out = str_with_capacity(n)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, s, 0, n)
    return out
}

internal fn strTrim(s: String) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut start = 0
    while (start < slen and charIsSpace(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, start))) {
        start = start + 1
    }
    let mut end = slen
    while (end > start and charIsSpace(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, end - 1))) {
        end = end - 1
    }
    let count = end - start
    let out = str_with_capacity(count)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, s, start, count)
    return out
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Building
//
// Every function in this section allocates its final size once and fills it in
// place. See the note at the top of the file: repeatedly replacing an owned
// accumulator still leaks every intermediate under today's ownership analysis.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

internal fn strRepeat(s: String, times: Int) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut n = times
    if (n < 0) { n = 0 }
    let out = str_with_capacity(slen * n)
    let mut w = 0
    let mut k = 0
    while (k < n) {
        let mut i = 0
        while (i < slen) {
            __builtin_string_put_byte(out, w, __builtin_string_byte_at(s, i))
            w = w + 1
            i = i + 1
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    return out
}

// **`width` counts characters.** It counted bytes, which lines a column up only
// while every value in it is ASCII: one `é` is two bytes, so a padded table with
// one accented name in it had that row a column short. Scalars are what a reader
// sees, so scalars are what a width means.
//
// Still not a *display* width -- an East Asian character occupies two terminal
// columns and a combining mark none -- which needs tables this library does not
// carry. For the alphabets a column of names is usually in, character count is
// the answer; for CJK, measure with your own table and pad by bytes.
//
// `pad` is a `Char` and therefore one byte, so the padding itself is ASCII.
internal fn strPadStart(s: String, width: Int, pad: Char) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut padLen = width - strScalarCount(s)
    if (padLen < 0) { padLen = 0 }
    let out = str_with_capacity(slen + padLen)
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < padLen) {
        __builtin_string_put_byte(out, i, pad)
        i = i + 1
    }
    strCopyRangeInto(out, padLen, s, 0, slen)
    return out
}

internal fn strPadEnd(s: String, width: Int, pad: Char) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut padLen = width - strScalarCount(s)
    if (padLen < 0) { padLen = 0 }
    let out = str_with_capacity(slen + padLen)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, s, 0, slen)
    let mut j = slen
    while (j < slen + padLen) {
        __builtin_string_put_byte(out, j, pad)
        j = j + 1
    }
    return out
}

// **ASCII, and only ASCII.** `charToUpper` maps `a`-`z` and leaves every other
// byte exactly as it was, so a multi-byte character passes through unchanged
// rather than being mangled -- the result is always valid UTF-8, and `é` is
// still `é`. Full case mapping needs Unicode tables this library does not carry,
// and one of its rules is that a string can change length: `ß` uppercases to
// `SS`, which no in-place byte loop can express.
//
// Written out twice rather than sharing a body with an `upper: Bool` flag.
// `return strMapChars(s, true)` is a pass-through -- the buffer was allocated a
// frame down, `aif_owns_call_result_at_node` declines it, and the caller gets no
// drop. Measured in exactly that shape: 1 allocated, 0 released. Every other
// builder here returns its own `str_with_capacity`, which is what makes them
// 1/1. The duplication is eight lines and it also drops a branch from the loop.
internal fn strToUpper(s: String) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let out = str_with_capacity(slen)
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        __builtin_string_put_byte(out, i, charToUpper(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, i)))
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

internal fn strToLower(s: String) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let out = str_with_capacity(slen)
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        __builtin_string_put_byte(out, i, charToLower(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, i)))
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// **Characters, not bytes.** Reversing the bytes of `"héllo"` produces a string
// whose second character is half of an `é` followed by the other half -- not a
// different string, an *invalid* one, and nothing downstream can recover from
// it. Each scalar moves as a unit instead, so the bytes of a character stay in
// order while the characters go the other way.
//
// A byte that is not part of a well-formed sequence moves on its own and
// unchanged, which makes this exact for ASCII and for binary held in a String,
// and leaves invalid input no worse than it arrived.
internal fn strReverse(s: String) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let out = str_with_capacity(slen)
    let mut read = 0
    let mut write = slen
    while (read < slen) {
        let mut width = strScalarWidthAt(s, read)
        if (width == 0) { width = 1 }
        write = write - width
        strCopyRangeInto(out, write, s, read, width)
        read = read + width
    }
    return out
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Replacing
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The first occurrence only. An absent or empty pattern returns a copy, so the
// result is owned on every path.
internal fn strReplaceFirst(s: String, old: String, new: String) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let oldLen = __builtin_string_len(old)
    let mut at = 0 - 1
    if (oldLen > 0) { at = strIndexOf(s, old) }

    if (at < 0) {
        let copy = str_with_capacity(slen)
        strCopyRangeInto(copy, 0, s, 0, slen)
        return copy
    }

    let newLen = __builtin_string_len(new)
    let tailStart = at + oldLen
    let tailLen = slen - tailStart
    let out = str_with_capacity(at + newLen + tailLen)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, s, 0, at)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, at, new, 0, newLen)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, at + newLen, s, tailStart, tailLen)
    return out
}

// Every occurrence, left to right, without rescanning what the replacement
// inserted -- so replacing "a" with "aa" terminates.
internal fn strReplaceAll(s: String, old: String, new: String) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let oldLen = __builtin_string_len(old)
    if (oldLen == 0) {
        let copy = str_with_capacity(slen)
        strCopyRangeInto(copy, 0, s, 0, slen)
        return copy
    }

    let newLen = __builtin_string_len(new)
    let count = strCountOf(s, old)
    let out = str_with_capacity(slen + count *(newLen - oldLen))
    let mut readAt = 0
    let mut writeAt = 0
    let mut matchAt = strIndexOf(s, old)
    while (matchAt >= 0) {
        let prefixLen = matchAt - readAt
        strCopyRangeInto(out, writeAt, s, readAt, prefixLen)
        writeAt = writeAt + prefixLen
        strCopyRangeInto(out, writeAt, new, 0, newLen)
        writeAt = writeAt + newLen
        readAt = matchAt + oldLen
        matchAt = strIndexOfFrom(s, old, readAt)
    }
    strCopyRangeInto(out, writeAt, s, readAt, slen - readAt)
    return out
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Splitting and joining
//
// `strSplit` returns `Vec<String>`, which is the reason it is here rather than
// in C. The C `str_split` returned a `StringArray*` -- a struct with no Prismio
// type, whose two allocations had to be released by calling `str_split_free`,
// which nothing in the ownership analysis knew about. A `Vec<String>` is an
// owned container the analysis already understands.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

internal fn strSplit(s: String, sep: Char) -> Vec<String> {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut out: Vec<String> = list_new()
    let mut start = 0
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        if (__builtin_string_byte_at(s, i) == sep) {
            // Twelve bytes or fewer live in the pair, as strSplitWhitespace's do.
            let partLen = i - start
            if (partLen <= 12) {
                list_push(out, __builtin_string_inline(s, start, partLen))
            } else {
                let part = str_with_capacity(partLen)
                strCopyRangeInto(part, 0, s, start, partLen)
                list_push(out, part)
            }
            start = i + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    let tailLen = slen - start
    if (tailLen <= 12) {
        list_push(out, __builtin_string_inline(s, start, tailLen))
    } else {
        let tail = str_with_capacity(tailLen)
        strCopyRangeInto(tail, 0, s, start, tailLen)
        list_push(out, tail)
    }
    return out
}

// Split on a multi-character separator. An empty separator yields the whole
// string as one part rather than exploding it into characters, because the
// alternative is an empty part between every pair of bytes.
internal fn strSplitOn(s: String, sep: String) -> Vec<String> {
    let mut out: Vec<String> = list_new()
    if (__builtin_string_len(sep) == 0) {
        let wholeLen = __builtin_string_len(s)
        let whole = str_with_capacity(wholeLen)
        strCopyRangeInto(whole, 0, s, 0, wholeLen)
        list_push(out, whole)
        return out
    }
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let seplen = __builtin_string_len(sep)
    let mut start = 0
    let mut at = strIndexOf(s, sep)
    while (at >= 0) {
        // Twelve bytes or fewer live in the pair, as strSplitWhitespace's do.
        let partLen = at - start
        if (partLen <= 12) {
            list_push(out, __builtin_string_inline(s, start, partLen))
        } else {
            let part = str_with_capacity(partLen)
            strCopyRangeInto(part, 0, s, start, partLen)
            list_push(out, part)
        }
        start = at + seplen
        at = strIndexOfFrom(s, sep, start)
    }
    let tailLen = slen - start
    if (tailLen <= 12) {
        list_push(out, __builtin_string_inline(s, start, tailLen))
    } else {
        let tail = str_with_capacity(tailLen)
        strCopyRangeInto(tail, 0, s, start, tailLen)
        list_push(out, tail)
    }
    return out
}

// Split on runs of whitespace, discarding empty parts. What a command line or a
// column-aligned file wants, and what `strSplit(s, ' ')` is not.
internal fn strSplitWhitespace(s: String) -> Vec<String> {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut out: Vec<String> = list_new()
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        while (i < slen and charIsSpace(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, i))) {
            i = i + 1
        }
        let start = i
        while (i < slen and charIsSpace(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, i)) == false) {
            i = i + 1
        }
        if (i > start) {
            let partLen = i - start
            // A word of twelve bytes or fewer lives in the pair itself, and a
            // `Vec<String>` keeps the pair: no allocation per word, which is
            // what libc++'s small strings in a `vector<string>` save C++.
            if (partLen <= 12) {
                list_push(out, __builtin_string_inline(s, start, partLen))
            } else {
                let part = str_with_capacity(partLen)
                strCopyRangeInto(part, 0, s, start, partLen)
                list_push(out, part)
            }
        }
    }
    return out
}

// One pass to size the result, one to fill it. Two traversals of the list beat
// the recursion this replaced, which allocated once per part and copied the
// whole accumulated tail each time.
internal fn strJoin(parts: Vec<String>, sep: String) -> String {
    let n = list_len(parts)
    let seplen = __builtin_string_len(sep)
    let mut total = 0
    if (n > 0) { total = seplen *(n - 1) }
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < n) {
        total = total + __builtin_string_len(list_get(parts, i))
        i = i + 1
    }
    let out = str_with_capacity(total)
    let mut w = 0
    let mut k = 0
    while (k < n) {
        if (k > 0) {
            strCopyRangeInto(out, w, sep, 0, seplen)
            w = w + seplen
        }
        // Read once per part. The copy loop used to call `list_get` per *byte*,
        // and a String read out of a container slot is re-measured with `strlen`:
        // 2.6 million calls joining 240,000 parts in `string_join`.
        let part = list_get(parts, k)
        let plen = __builtin_string_len(part)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, w, part, 0, plen)
        w = w + plen
        k = k + 1
    }
    return out
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Numbers
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------



// `None` rather than a sentinel when the text is not an integer.
//
// This is the operation the C `str_to_int` could not express: it was `atoi`,
// which returns 0 for "0" and 0 for "banana" and gives the caller no way to tell
// them apart. REQUIREMENTS 14's whole argument against sentinels is this
// function.
internal fn strParseInt(s: String) -> Option<Int> {
    return strParseIntRadix(s, 10)
}

// Compared, not divided.
//
// Sizing the buffer used to be a second division pass over the value -- one
// divide per digit, before the fill loop did them all again. A chain of
// comparisons against the powers of ten answers the same question with no
// division through 18 digits, and it is the pass that made integer formatting
// 2.1x Rust rather than the divides inside the fill. The last boundary uses one
// division because 10^19 is outside signed I64 and cannot be an exact literal
// until integer-literal typing grows a U64 case.
private fn strDigitCount(value: U64) -> Int {
    if (value < 10) { return 1 }
    if (value < 100) { return 2 }
    if (value < 1000) { return 3 }
    if (value < 10000) { return 4 }
    if (value < 100000) { return 5 }
    if (value < 1000000) { return 6 }
    if (value < 10000000) { return 7 }
    if (value < 100000000) { return 8 }
    if (value < 1000000000) { return 9 }
    if (value < 10000000000) { return 10 }
    if (value < 100000000000) { return 11 }
    if (value < 1000000000000) { return 12 }
    if (value < 10000000000000) { return 13 }
    if (value < 100000000000000) { return 14 }
    if (value < 1000000000000000) { return 15 }
    if (value < 10000000000000000) { return 16 }
    if (value < 100000000000000000) { return 17 }
    if (value < 1000000000000000000) { return 18 }
    if (value / 10 < 1000000000000000000) { return 19 }
    return 20
}

// Filled back to front into one allocation. The recursion this replaced cost an
// allocation and a full copy per digit -- O(d^2) bytes for a d-digit number --
// which is why formatting integers was 2.8x the C `int_to_str`.
// Two digits per division, from a flat table of "00".."99".
//
// The division is the cost -- an integer divide is tens of cycles where the byte
// stores are one -- so halving the count of them is most of the operation.
// Rust's integer `Display` is built the same way, and the 2.13x it held over a
// digit-at-a-time loop was almost entirely this.
private fn strTwoDigitTable() -> String {
    return "00010203040506070809101112131415161718192021222324252627282930313233343536373839404142434445464748495051525354555657585960616263646566676869707172737475767778798081828384858687888990919293949596979899"
}

internal fn strFromUnsigned(value: U64) -> String {
    let digits = strDigitCount(value)
    let out = str_with_capacity(digits)
    let table = strTwoDigitTable()
    let mut v = value
    let mut i = digits - 1
    while (v >= 100) {
        let pair =((v % 100) as Int) * 2
        __builtin_string_put_byte(out, i, __builtin_string_byte_at(table, pair + 1))
        __builtin_string_put_byte(out, i - 1, __builtin_string_byte_at(table, pair))
        i = i - 2
        v = v / 100
    }
    // One or two digits left, and the same table serves both.
    let pair =((v % 100) as Int) * 2
    __builtin_string_put_byte(out, i, __builtin_string_byte_at(table, pair + 1))
    if (i > 0) {
        __builtin_string_put_byte(out, i - 1, __builtin_string_byte_at(table, pair))
    }
    return out
}

// Decimal text for an Int. Every 32-bit result is at most eleven bytes including
// the sign, so the compiler formats it directly into String's twelve-byte inline
// payload. U64 keeps the allocating builder above because it may need 20 bytes.
internal fn strFromInt(value: Int) -> String {
    return __builtin_string_from_int(value)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Predicates and checked access
//
// The `Option`-returning forms here are **additions, not replacements**.
// `strIndexOf` still answers -1 and `charAt` still answers NUL, because a
// sentinel costs a register where `Option` costs a tagged struct and an
// allocation (see the representation note in std/option.psm), and because the
// compiler reads them that way at 848 call sites. What these add is the spelling
// for a caller who would rather be *made* to handle the absent case --
// REQUIREMENTS 14's argument, offered rather than imposed.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Empty, or nothing but the four space bytes. Distinct from `strIsEmpty`, which
// is a length test: "   " is blank and is not empty.
internal fn strIsBlank(s: String) -> Bool {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        if (charIsSpace(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, i)) == false) { return false }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return true
}

// ASCII-folded equality. Not a Unicode case fold: `charToLower` maps A-Z and
// nothing else, so this answers the question a flag or a header name asks and
// not the one a natural-language comparison does.
internal fn strEqualsIgnoreCase(a: String, b: String) -> Bool {
    let alen = __builtin_string_len(a)
    if (alen != __builtin_string_len(b)) { return false }
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < alen) {
        let ca = charToLower(__builtin_string_byte_at(a, i))
        let cb = charToLower(__builtin_string_byte_at(b, i))
        if (ca != cb) { return false }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return true
}

internal fn strGet(s: String, index: Int) -> Option<Char> {
    if (index < 0 or index >= __builtin_string_len(s)) { return Option<Char>.None }
    return Option<Char>.Some(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, index))
}

// NUL on an empty string, for the same reason `charAt` answers NUL out of
// range: a Prismio String cannot contain one, so the answer is unambiguous.
internal fn strFirst(s: String) -> Char {
    if (__builtin_string_len(s) == 0) { return '\0' }
    return __builtin_string_byte_at(s, 0)
}

internal fn strLast(s: String) -> Char {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    if (slen == 0) { return '\0' }
    return __builtin_string_byte_at(s, slen - 1)
}

internal fn strFind(s: String, needle: String) -> Option<Int> {
    let at = strIndexOf(s, needle)
    if (at < 0) { return Option<Int>.None }
    return Option<Int>.Some(at)
}

internal fn strFindFrom(s: String, needle: String, from: Int) -> Option<Int> {
    let at = strIndexOfFrom(s, needle, from)
    if (at < 0) { return Option<Int>.None }
    return Option<Int>.Some(at)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Single-byte search
//
// A separate family rather than `strIndexOf(s, strFromChar(c))`, because that
// spelling allocates a one-byte String per call and then hands the general
// substring machinery a needle it has a direct instruction for. `str_find_byte`
// is libc's vectorised scan.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

internal fn strIndexOfCharFrom(s: String, needle: Char, from: Int) -> Int {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut start = from
    if (start < 0) { start = 0 }
    if (start >= slen) { return 0 - 1 }
    return str_find_byte(s, start, needle)
}

internal fn strIndexOfChar(s: String, needle: Char) -> Int {
    return strIndexOfCharFrom(s, needle, 0)
}

internal fn strLastIndexOfChar(s: String, needle: Char) -> Int {
    let mut i = __builtin_string_len(s)
    while (i > 0) {
        if (__builtin_string_byte_at(s, i - 1) == needle) { return i - 1 }
        i = i - 1
    }
    return 0 - 1
}

internal fn strContainsChar(s: String, needle: Char) -> Bool {
    return strIndexOfChar(s, needle) >= 0
}

internal fn strStartsWithChar(s: String, prefix: Char) -> Bool {
    if (__builtin_string_len(s) == 0) { return false }
    return __builtin_string_byte_at(s, 0) == prefix
}

internal fn strEndsWithChar(s: String, suffix: Char) -> Bool {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    if (slen == 0) { return false }
    return __builtin_string_byte_at(s, slen - 1) == suffix
}

internal fn strCountOfChar(s: String, needle: Char) -> Int {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut hits = 0
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        if (__builtin_string_byte_at(s, i) == needle) { hits = hits + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return hits
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Producers
//
// Same rule as the Building section above: allocate the final size once and fill
// it. None of these grows a buffer, because an assignment releases nothing.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The first byte uppercased, the rest untouched. Deliberately not "the rest
// lowercased": that is a different operation, it destroys `"McDonald"`, and a
// caller who wants it can spell `s.toLower().capitalize()`.
internal fn strCapitalize(s: String) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let out = str_with_capacity(slen)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, s, 0, slen)
    if (slen > 0) {
        __builtin_string_put_byte(out, 0, charToUpper(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, 0)))
    }
    return out
}

// An odd amount of padding puts the extra byte at the end, so that centring a
// run of strings to the same width keeps their left edges aligned.
// Characters either side, for the reason `strPadStart` gives. An odd remainder
// goes to the right, so `strPadCenter("ab", 5, '-')` is `-ab--`.
internal fn strPadCenter(s: String, width: Int, pad: Char) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut total = width - strScalarCount(s)
    if (total < 0) { total = 0 }
    let left = total / 2
    let outLen = slen + total
    let out = str_with_capacity(outLen)
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < left) {
        __builtin_string_put_byte(out, i, pad)
        i = i + 1
    }
    strCopyRangeInto(out, left, s, 0, slen)
    let mut j = left + slen
    while (j < outLen) {
        __builtin_string_put_byte(out, j, pad)
        j = j + 1
    }
    return out
}

// Trim any byte that appears in `set`, from both ends. `strTrim` is this with
// the four space bytes, kept separate because it is the common case and needs no
// inner scan per byte.
internal fn strTrimChars(s: String, set: String) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut start = 0
    while (start < slen and strContainsChar(set, __builtin_string_byte_at(s, start))) {
        start = start + 1
    }
    let mut end = slen
    while (end > start and strContainsChar(set, __builtin_string_byte_at(s, end - 1))) {
        end = end - 1
    }
    let count = end - start
    let out = str_with_capacity(count)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, s, start, count)
    return out
}

// `at` past the end appends rather than failing, matching how every range in
// this file is clamped rather than refused.
internal fn strInsert(s: String, at: Int, other: String) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let olen = __builtin_string_len(other)
    let mut cut = at
    if (cut < 0) { cut = 0 }
    if (cut > slen) { cut = slen }
    let out = str_with_capacity(slen + olen)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, s, 0, cut)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, cut, other, 0, olen)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, cut + olen, s, cut, slen - cut)
    return out
}

internal fn strRemoveRange(s: String, start: Int, length: Int) -> String {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut first = start
    if (first < 0) { first = 0 }
    if (first > slen) { first = slen }
    let mut count = length
    if (count < 0) { count = 0 }
    if (first + count > slen) { count = slen - first }
    let out = str_with_capacity(slen - count)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, s, 0, first)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, first, s, first + count, slen - first - count)
    return out
}

// `None` when the prefix is absent -- which is the difference from
// `strStartsWith` plus `strSubstring`, where the caller has to remember to test
// first and gets a silently wrong slice if they do not.
internal fn strStripPrefix(s: String, prefix: String) -> Option<String> {
    if (strStartsWith(s, prefix) == false) { return Option<String>.None }
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let plen = __builtin_string_len(prefix)
    let out = str_with_capacity(slen - plen)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, s, plen, slen - plen)
    return Option<String>.Some(out)
}

internal fn strStripSuffix(s: String, suffix: String) -> Option<String> {
    if (strEndsWith(s, suffix) == false) { return Option<String>.None }
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let sufLen = __builtin_string_len(suffix)
    let out = str_with_capacity(slen - sufLen)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, s, 0, slen - sufLen)
    return Option<String>.Some(out)
}

// Not `strSplit(s, '\n')`, and the two differences are the ones that bite when
// reading a file: a trailing newline *ends* the last line rather than starting
// an empty one, and a CRLF file does not leave a '\r' on every element.
internal fn strLines(s: String) -> Vec<String> {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut out: Vec<String> = list_new()
    let mut start = 0
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        if (__builtin_string_byte_at(s, i) == '\n') {
            let mut end = i
            if (end > start and __builtin_string_byte_at(s, end - 1) == '\r') { end = end - 1 }
            // Twelve bytes or fewer live in the pair, as strSplitWhitespace's do.
            let partLen = end - start
            if (partLen <= 12) {
                list_push(out, __builtin_string_inline(s, start, partLen))
            } else {
                let part = str_with_capacity(partLen)
                strCopyRangeInto(part, 0, s, start, partLen)
                list_push(out, part)
            }
            start = i + 1
        }
        i = i + 1
    }
    if (start < slen) {
        let mut tailEnd = slen
        if (__builtin_string_byte_at(s, tailEnd - 1) == '\r') { tailEnd = tailEnd - 1 }
        let tailLen = tailEnd - start
        if (tailLen <= 12) {
            list_push(out, __builtin_string_inline(s, start, tailLen))
        } else {
            let tail = str_with_capacity(tailLen)
            strCopyRangeInto(tail, 0, s, start, tailLen)
            list_push(out, tail)
        }
    }
    return out
}

// One `Char` per **byte**, because a `Char` is a byte. For text that may not be
// ASCII this is not the character list its name suggests -- `strScalars` is,
// and returns `Vec<Int>` because a code point does not fit a `Char`.
internal fn strChars(s: String) -> Vec<Char> {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut out: Vec<Char> = list_new()
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        list_push(out, __builtin_string_byte_at(s, i))
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

internal fn strBytes(s: String) -> Vec<Int> {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut out: Vec<Int> = list_new()
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        list_push(out, __builtin_string_byte_at(s, i) as Int)
        i = i + 1
    }
    return out
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Closure-driven scans
//
// `strEach` is deliberately absent: a closure's return type has no `Void`
// spelling yet, so `|c: Char| println(c)` does not typecheck. Iterate with
// `for c in s` instead, which is the form that needs no closure at all.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

internal fn strCountIf<F>(s: String, keep: F) -> Int {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut hits = 0
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        if (keep(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, i))) { hits = hits + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return hits
}

internal fn strIndexWhere<F>(s: String, keep: F) -> Int {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        if (keep(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, i))) { return i }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return 0 - 1
}

internal fn strAllChars<F>(s: String, keep: F) -> Bool {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        if (keep(__builtin_string_byte_at(s, i)) == false) { return false }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return true
}

internal fn strAnyChars<F>(s: String, keep: F) -> Bool {
    return strIndexWhere(s, keep) >= 0
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Formatting and parsing
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

internal fn strFromBool(value: Bool) -> String {
    if (value) {
        let yes = str_with_capacity(4)
        __builtin_string_put_byte(yes, 0, 't')
        __builtin_string_put_byte(yes, 1, 'r')
        __builtin_string_put_byte(yes, 2, 'u')
        __builtin_string_put_byte(yes, 3, 'e')
        return yes
    }
    let no = str_with_capacity(5)
    __builtin_string_put_byte(no, 0, 'f')
    __builtin_string_put_byte(no, 1, 'a')
    __builtin_string_put_byte(no, 2, 'l')
    __builtin_string_put_byte(no, 3, 's')
    __builtin_string_put_byte(no, 4, 'e')
    return no
}

// Exactly the two spellings `strFromBool` produces, case-insensitively. Not
// "1"/"0" and not "yes"/"no": a parser that accepts more than its printer emits
// is a parser whose round trip is untestable.
internal fn strParseBool(s: String) -> Option<Bool> {
    if (strEqualsIgnoreCase(s, "true")) { return Option<Bool>.Some(true) }
    if (strEqualsIgnoreCase(s, "false")) { return Option<Bool>.Some(false) }
    return Option<Bool>.None
}

// **Not correctly rounded, and that is a documented limit rather than a bug to
// find later.** The digits are accumulated as an integer and divided by a power
// of ten once, so the result is within one ulp but is not always the nearest
// double to the decimal text -- a correctly-rounded parser needs big-integer
// arithmetic (Clinger/Eisel-Lemire) that this library does not have yet. Use it
// for configuration and input; do not use it where a decimal literal must
// round-trip bit-for-bit.
//
// Rejects what it cannot represent rather than guessing: empty text, a lone
// sign, a lone dot, any non-digit, and more than 18 significant digits.
private extern fn str_double_valid(text: String borrow) -> Int
private extern fn str_double_value(text: String borrow) -> Float

// **Reads back what `strFromFloat` writes, and nothing else.**
//
// The parser this replaces was source-level, and wrong three ways: its mantissa
// accumulated in an `Int` while its own guard allowed eighteen digits, so a long
// number wrapped and came back as a `Some` holding a different value; it divided
// by ten once per decimal place, which lands near the nearest double rather than
// on it; and it had no exponent case, so `1e+21` -- which is exactly what
// `strFromFloat` produces for a large value -- did not parse. `strtod` is
// correctly rounded and is the function the formatter checks its own candidates
// against, so the pair round-trips by construction.
//
// Stricter than C: the whole string must be a number, so `"1.5kg"` and `" 1.5"`
// are `None` rather than 1.5. `"inf"`, `"-inf"` and `"nan"` parse, because
// `strFromFloat` writes them.
internal fn strParseFloat(s: String) -> Option<Float> {
    if (str_double_valid(s) == 0) { return Option<Float>.None }
    return Option<Float>.Some(str_double_value(s))
}

// The parse, or the caller's number. For the very common case where a bad value
// means "use the default" and the `Option` would only ever be fed to `optionOr`.
internal fn strParseIntOr(s: String, fallback: Int) -> Int {
    return optionOr(strParseInt(s), fallback)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// `Vec<String>` receivers
//
// Free functions rather than an `impl`: `impl Vec<String>` is a generic `impl`,
// which the parser rejects on purpose (src/parse/decl.psm), and the method
// rewrite `x.f(a)` -> `f(x, a)` never needed an `impl` block to work. So
// `parts.join(", ")` resolves to this with no new machinery at all.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

public fn join(parts: Vec<String>, sep: String) -> String {
    return strJoin(parts, sep)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// v0.1 3.2 -- the method surface
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `text.trim()` rather than `strTrim(text)`. Most of these delegate to the
// `str*` function above and add nothing: `x.f(a)` is rewritten to `f(x, a)`
// before overload resolution, so a method here *is* the free function, reached
// by a second name.
//
// **The prefix is not a style choice and this block does not retire it.** There
// is no namespacing yet (v0.1 3.5), so `strTrim` is what stops this module's
// names from colliding with an application's. Until `string.trim` is spellable,
// an unprefixed free function called `trim` would claim that name for every
// program that imports this file, so the rename has to happen once, deliberately,
// rather than as a side effect of adding methods.
//
// **Five names have already made that move, and were forced to.** `equals`,
// `concat`, `slice`, `charAt` and `compare` are what the String operators lower
// to, which made them compiler contracts rather than only API -- and a lowering
// target has to be reachable from any program that writes the operator, so the
// prefixed spelling could never be made `internal` while it was the target. They
// carry their bodies here and have no `str*` twin left; the swap this comment
// used to anticipate has happened for exactly the set that could not wait.
//
// Curated rather than exhaustive. Every method here costs a function in every
// binary that imports this module -- there is no dead-function elimination in
// the frontend -- so the search internals (`strTwoWayIndexOfFrom`,
// `strMaximalSuffix`, `strSearchByteRank`) and the digit tables deliberately
// have no method: they are implementation, not surface.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Float text
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// **The shortest decimal text, at most fifteen significant digits.**
//
// `Float` used to be the one type a program could print and parse but never
// convert: there was no `strFromFloat`, so a float could not be joined into a
// line, written to a file, used as a map key's text, or built into a message.
// The formatting itself is C, because finding the shortest form means parsing
// candidates back, and `strtod` is where that lives -- see
// `prismio_format_double` in runtime/lang_runtime.c. Fifteen is DBL_DIG, so
// every decimal literal prints as written and `0.1 + 0.2` is `0.3`; a value
// that needs more digits to round-trip, like `1.0 / 3.0`, prints fifteen.
//
// **`print(f)` writes exactly this text**, because both go through that one
// function. A value that reaches the console and the same value converted to a
// String must not disagree.
//
// Wrapping an `extern` that produces is a pass-through, which normally costs the
// caller its drop (see the note over `concat`). It does not here, and the reason
// is the argument: the leak needs the callee's allocation to be in both the
// return's points-to set and a parameter's, and a `Float` parameter has no
// points-to set at all. `tests/test_135_string_production.psm` measures it.
private extern fn str_from_double(value: Float) -> String produce(free)
private extern fn str_from_double_fixed(value: Float, decimals: Int) -> String produce(free)

internal fn strFromFloat(value: Float) -> String {
    return str_from_double(value)
}

// A fixed number of decimals, for a column rather than a value -- prices,
// percentages, a table of timings: `strFromFloatFixed(1.0 / 3.0, 2)` is `0.33`,
// and `strFromFloatFixed(2.0, 2)` keeps its zeros as `2.00`.
// `decimals` is clamped to 0..30.
internal fn strFromFloatFixed(value: Float, decimals: Int) -> String {
    return str_from_double_fixed(value, decimals)
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// UTF-8
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// **A String is bytes, and these are the functions that read it as text.**
//
// `Char` is a byte in 0.1 and `length`, `charAt`, `substring` and the operators
// that lower to them are byte-indexed. That is the right default for a compiler
// -- it is what makes a scan one comparison per byte -- and it is a trap for
// anything else, because a byte index can fall inside a character. Nothing here
// changes those; they add the second reading, so a program that handles user
// text can ask for scalars and get an answer that is right for every language.
//
// A **scalar** is a Unicode code point, in the range 0..0x10FFFF minus the
// surrogates, held in an `Int` because a `Char` cannot hold one.

// How many bytes the scalar starting with this byte occupies: 0xxxxxxx is one,
// 110xxxxx two, 1110xxxx three, 11110xxx four. A continuation byte (10xxxxxx) is
// not a lead and answers 0, which is what makes "is this a boundary" the same
// question as "is this a lead".
private fn strUtf8LeadWidth(lead: Int) -> Int {
    if (lead < 128) { return 1 }
    if (lead < 192) { return 0 }
    if (lead < 224) { return 2 }
    if (lead < 240) { return 3 }
    if (lead < 248) { return 4 }
    return 0
}

private fn strUtf8ContinuationsOk(s: String, at: Int, slen: Int, count: Int) -> Bool {
    let mut k = 1
    while (k <= count) {
        if (at + k >= slen) { return false }
        let b = strByteAt(s, at + k)
        if (b < 128 or b > 191) { return false }
        k = k + 1
    }
    return true
}

// The scalar beginning at byte `at`, or -1 when the bytes there are not a
// well-formed sequence.
//
// **Well-formed is narrower than decodable**, and the three cases it excludes
// are the ones that have been security bugs elsewhere. An *overlong* encoding
// spells an ASCII character in two or more bytes, which is how a filter looking
// for `/` or `.` gets walked past. A *surrogate* (U+D800..U+DFFF) is not a
// scalar at all -- it is half of a UTF-16 pair and has no business in UTF-8.
// And nothing above U+10FFFF is Unicode. Each answers -1 rather than a number no
// caller can act on.
internal fn strScalarAt(s: String, at: Int) -> Int {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    if (at < 0 or at >= slen) { return 0 - 1 }

    let lead = strByteAt(s, at)
    let width = strUtf8LeadWidth(lead)
    if (width == 0) { return 0 - 1 }
    if (width == 1) { return lead }
    if (strUtf8ContinuationsOk(s, at, slen, width - 1) == false) { return 0 - 1 }

    let mut scalar = lead - 192
    if (width == 3) { scalar = lead - 224 }
    if (width == 4) { scalar = lead - 240 }
    let mut k = 1
    while (k < width) {
        scalar = scalar * 64 + (strByteAt(s, at + k) - 128)
        k = k + 1
    }

    if (width == 2 and scalar < 128) { return 0 - 1 }
    if (width == 3 and scalar < 2048) { return 0 - 1 }
    if (width == 4 and scalar < 65536) { return 0 - 1 }
    if (scalar >= 55296 and scalar <= 57343) { return 0 - 1 }
    if (scalar > 1114111) { return 0 - 1 }
    return scalar
}

// The width in bytes of the scalar at `at`, or 0 when there is not one there.
// Walking a String by scalar is this in a loop: `i = i + strScalarWidthAt(s, i)`.
internal fn strScalarWidthAt(s: String, at: Int) -> Int {
    if (strScalarAt(s, at) < 0) { return 0 }
    return strUtf8LeadWidth(strByteAt(s, at))
}

// Whether a byte index falls between characters rather than inside one. The
// question to ask before `substring`, whose indices are bytes.
internal fn strIsCharBoundary(s: String, at: Int) -> Bool {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    if (at == 0 or at == slen) { return true }
    if (at < 0 or at > slen) { return false }
    let b = strByteAt(s, at)
    return b < 128 or b > 191
}

internal fn strIsAscii(s: String) -> Bool {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        if (strByteAt(s, i) > 127) { return false }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return true
}

// Whether every byte is part of a well-formed sequence. Worth asking once at the
// boundary where text enters a program -- a file, an argument, a socket -- after
// which the rest of this section can be used without checking again.
internal fn strIsValidUtf8(s: String) -> Bool {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        let width = strScalarWidthAt(s, i)
        if (width == 0) { return false }
        i = i + width
    }
    return true
}

// How many characters, as opposed to `length`'s bytes.
//
// Counts lead bytes rather than decoding, so it costs one comparison per byte
// and answers for invalid input too: a byte that is not a continuation counts as
// one character. That is what makes it usable for padding, where refusing to
// answer would be worse than approximating.
internal fn strScalarCount(s: String) -> Int {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut count = 0
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        let b = strByteAt(s, i)
        if (b < 128 or b > 191) { count = count + 1 }
        i = i + 1
    }
    return count
}

// Every scalar, in order. An ill-formed byte becomes U+FFFD, the replacement
// character, and advances one byte -- the substitution the Unicode standard
// specifies, so that decoding never fails and never silently drops input.
internal fn strScalars(s: String) -> Vec<Int> {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut out: Vec<Int> = list_new()
    let mut i = 0
    while (i < slen) {
        let scalar = strScalarAt(s, i)
        if (scalar < 0) {
            list_push(out, 65533)
            i = i + 1
        } else {
            list_push(out, scalar)
            i = i + strUtf8LeadWidth(strByteAt(s, i))
        }
    }
    return out
}

// One scalar as its UTF-8 bytes. A value that is not a scalar -- negative, a
// surrogate, or above U+10FFFF -- encodes as U+FFFD rather than as bytes no
// reader could decode.
public fn strFromScalar(scalar: Int) -> String {
    let mut value = scalar
    if (value < 0 or value > 1114111) { value = 65533 }
    if (value >= 55296 and value <= 57343) { value = 65533 }

    if (value < 128) {
        let one = str_with_capacity(1)
        __builtin_string_put_byte(one, 0, value as Char)
        return one
    }
    if (value < 2048) {
        let two = str_with_capacity(2)
        __builtin_string_put_byte(two, 0, (192 + value / 64) as Char)
        __builtin_string_put_byte(two, 1, (128 + value % 64) as Char)
        return two
    }
    if (value < 65536) {
        let three = str_with_capacity(3)
        __builtin_string_put_byte(three, 0, (224 + value / 4096) as Char)
        __builtin_string_put_byte(three, 1, (128 + (value / 64) % 64) as Char)
        __builtin_string_put_byte(three, 2, (128 + value % 64) as Char)
        return three
    }
    let four = str_with_capacity(4)
    __builtin_string_put_byte(four, 0, (240 + value / 262144) as Char)
    __builtin_string_put_byte(four, 1, (128 + (value / 4096) % 64) as Char)
    __builtin_string_put_byte(four, 2, (128 + (value / 64) % 64) as Char)
    __builtin_string_put_byte(four, 3, (128 + value % 64) as Char)
    return four
}

// `count` characters starting at the `start`-th, both counted in scalars rather
// than bytes. The safe form of `substring` for text that is not known to be
// ASCII: it cannot cut a character in half, because it never chooses a byte
// index that is not a boundary.
// The byte offset of the `index`-th character, or the end of the string.
//
// **Walking is here rather than in the function below**, and that is not
// factoring for its own sake. `strSubstring` returns a *view* of its argument,
// and the analysis carries "returns a view of parameter 0" through a direct
// return but not out of a function that loops first -- so a version of
// `strScalarSubstring` with these loops in its body read 1 allocated / 0
// released for `strScalarSubstring(owned, 1, 4)`: the caller's drop of `owned`
// was declined, and the view that declined it took nothing.
private fn strScalarOffset(s: String, index: Int) -> Int {
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    let mut at = 0
    let mut seen = 0
    while (seen < index and at < slen) {
        let mut width = strScalarWidthAt(s, at)
        if (width == 0) { width = 1 }
        at = at + width
        seen = seen + 1
    }
    return at
}

internal fn strScalarSubstring(s: String, start: Int, count: Int) -> String {
    let first = strScalarOffset(s, start)
    let last = strScalarOffset(s, start + count)

    // **A copy, where `strSubstring` would hand back a view.**
    //
    // A view aliases its source, and the analysis keeps the source alive by
    // declining the caller's drop of it -- which it can do for `strSubstring`
    // itself, whose result is `__builtin_string_view` of a parameter and says so.
    // It cannot follow that fact out of a function that passes the parameter to
    // another one on the way, and the measurement is unambiguous:
    // `strScalarSubstring(owned, 1, 4)` read 1 allocated / 0 released. Owning the
    // result costs one allocation and is the rule the header of this file states
    // for every producer here.
    let out = str_with_capacity(last - first)
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, s, first, last - first)
    return out
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Radix formatting
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

private fn strRadixDigits() -> String {
    return "0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz"
}

// Base 2 through 36, lowercase, no prefix -- a caller wanting `0x` writes it.
// A radix outside that range is a programming error rather than a runtime one,
// and is clamped rather than made every call site handle an `Option`.
internal fn strFromU64Radix(value: U64, radix: Int) -> String {
    let mut base = radix
    if (base < 2) { base = 2 }
    if (base > 36) { base = 36 }
    let wide = base as U64

    let mut digits = 1
    let mut counting = value
    while (counting >= wide) {
        counting = counting / wide
        digits = digits + 1
    }

    let out = str_with_capacity(digits)
    let table = strRadixDigits()
    let mut remaining = value
    let mut at = digits - 1
    loop {
        __builtin_string_put_byte(out, at, table.charAt((remaining % wide) as Int))
        if (at == 0) { break }
        remaining = remaining / wide
        at = at - 1
    }
    return out
}

internal fn strFromIntRadix(value: Int, radix: Int) -> String {
    if (value >= 0) { return strFromU64Radix(value as U64, radix) }

    // The magnitude through U64, so that the most negative Int -- whose positive
    // is not an Int -- formats like any other rather than overflowing on the way.
    let magnitude = (0 as U64) - (value as I64 as U64)
    let digitsText = strFromU64Radix(magnitude, radix)
    let width = __builtin_string_len(digitsText)
    let out = str_with_capacity(width + 1)
    __builtin_string_put_byte(out, 0, '-')
    strCopyRangeInto(out, 1, digitsText, 0, width)
    return out
}

internal fn strHex(value: U64) -> String { return strFromU64Radix(value, 16) }
internal fn strOctal(value: U64) -> String { return strFromU64Radix(value, 8) }
internal fn strBinary(value: U64) -> String { return strFromU64Radix(value, 2) }

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Parsing, with the range checked
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

// The largest U64, written as a subtraction because an integer literal is typed
// `Int` and 18446744073709551615 is not one. The same trick names the I64 bounds
// below, and it is exact: unsigned wrapping is defined.
private fn strMaxU64() -> U64 {
    return (0 as U64) - (1 as U64)
}

private fn strDigitValueIn(c: Int, radix: Int) -> Int {
    let mut value = 0 - 1
    if (c >= 48 and c <= 57) { value = c - 48 }
    if (c >= 97 and c <= 122) { value = c - 97 + 10 }
    if (c >= 65 and c <= 90) { value = c - 65 + 10 }
    if (value < 0 or value >= radix) { return 0 - 1 }
    return value
}

// **`None` on overflow, and that is the fix this replaces.** The accumulator ran
// `acc * 10 + digit` with no bound, so `"99999999999"` came back as a `Some`
// holding a wrapped number -- a parse that reported success and produced a value
// the text does not say. Every digit is now checked before it is added: against
// the largest value that can still be multiplied, and then against what is left
// after multiplying.
private fn strParseU64Body(s: String, at: Int, slen: Int, radix: Int) -> Option<U64> {
    if (at >= slen) { return Option<U64>.None }
    let wide = radix as U64
    let ceiling = strMaxU64() / wide

    let mut acc = 0 as U64
    let mut i = at
    while (i < slen) {
        let digit = strDigitValueIn(strByteAt(s, i), radix)
        if (digit < 0) { return Option<U64>.None }
        if (acc > ceiling) { return Option<U64>.None }
        acc = acc * wide
        if (acc > strMaxU64() - (digit as U64)) { return Option<U64>.None }
        acc = acc + (digit as U64)
        i = i + 1
    }
    return Option<U64>.Some(acc)
}

// A leading `+` is accepted and a leading `-` is not: an unsigned parse of a
// negative number has no answer, and wrapping to a large positive one is the
// class of bug this whole section exists to remove.
internal fn strParseU64Radix(s: String, radix: Int) -> Option<U64> {
    if (radix < 2 or radix > 36) { return Option<U64>.None }
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    if (slen == 0) { return Option<U64>.None }
    if (strByteAt(s, 0) == 43) { return strParseU64Body(s, 1, slen, radix) }
    return strParseU64Body(s, 0, slen, radix)
}

internal fn strParseU64(s: String) -> Option<U64> {
    return strParseU64Radix(s, 10)
}

private fn strMaxI64Magnitude() -> U64 {
    return strMaxU64() / (2 as U64)
}

internal fn strParseI64Radix(s: String, radix: Int) -> Option<I64> {
    if (radix < 2 or radix > 36) { return Option<I64>.None }
    let slen = __builtin_string_len(s)
    if (slen == 0) { return Option<I64>.None }

    let negative = strByteAt(s, 0) == 45
    let mut start = 0
    if (negative or strByteAt(s, 0) == 43) { start = 1 }
    if (start >= slen) { return Option<I64>.None }

    let magnitude = strParseU64Body(s, start, slen, radix)
    if (optionIsNone(magnitude)) { return Option<I64>.None }
    let value = optionOr(magnitude, 0 as U64)

    if (negative == false) {
        if (value > strMaxI64Magnitude()) { return Option<I64>.None }
        return Option<I64>.Some(value as I64)
    }
    // The negative range reaches one further than the positive one, and its end
    // has no positive counterpart to negate -- so it is named rather than
    // computed, which is also what keeps the negation below in range.
    if (value > strMaxI64Magnitude() + (1 as U64)) { return Option<I64>.None }
    if (value == strMaxI64Magnitude() + (1 as U64)) {
        return Option<I64>.Some((0 as I64) - (strMaxI64Magnitude() as I64) - (1 as I64))
    }
    return Option<I64>.Some((0 as I64) - (value as I64))
}

internal fn strParseI64(s: String) -> Option<I64> {
    return strParseI64Radix(s, 10)
}

internal fn strParseIntRadix(s: String, radix: Int) -> Option<Int> {
    let wide = strParseI64Radix(s, radix)
    if (optionIsNone(wide)) { return Option<Int>.None }
    let value = optionOr(wide, 0 as I64)
    // `Int` is i32 (RUNTIME.md), so a value that fits an I64 and not an Int is a
    // number this function cannot return. It says so rather than truncating.
    if (value > 2147483647 as I64) { return Option<Int>.None }
    if (value < (0 - 2147483647 - 1) as I64) { return Option<Int>.None }
    return Option<Int>.Some(value as Int)
}

impl String {
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The five the operators lower to
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    //
    // `a + b`, `s[i]`, `s[a..b]` and the four orderings are rewritten into these
    // by src/sema/checker.psm, so they are compiler contracts and not only
    // surface -- which is why they are also the five with no `str*` function
    // left to delegate to. The operator needed a target that is not part of this
    // module's public prefix, and moving the bodies here is what let the
    // prefixed names go.
    //
    // **They carry their implementations rather than delegating, and that is
    // rule 2 of this file's header rather than a preference.** A function whose
    // `return` is another function's allocation is a pass-through: its caller
    // gets no drop as soon as an argument is itself owned, because the callee's
    // one site is then in both the return's points-to set and the parameter's.
    // Measured over 1,000 iterations of `let t = f("a","b"); let u = f(t,"c")`:
    // delegating reads **2,001 allocated / 1 released / 2,000 leaked**, owning
    // the body reads **2,001 / 2,001 / 0**. A version of `concat` that forwarded
    // to a private helper would leak once per `+` in every program.
    //
    // Two to six parts in **one construction**: no allocation when the result
    // fits in the inline pair, and one allocation otherwise. `a + b + c` lowers
    // to one call rather than a nested join, so there is no intermediate to
    // allocate and release at all. Six is where it stops because `strJoin` is
    // the right answer past that.
    public fn concat(self, other: String) -> String {
        let alen = __builtin_string_len(self)
        let blen = __builtin_string_len(other)
        let total = alen + blen
        if (total <= 12) { return __builtin_string_concat_inline2(self, other) }
        let out = str_with_capacity(total)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, self, 0, alen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen, other, 0, blen)
        return out
    }

    public fn concat(self, b: String, c: String) -> String {
        let alen = __builtin_string_len(self)
        let blen = __builtin_string_len(b)
        let clen = __builtin_string_len(c)
        let total = alen + blen + clen
        if (total <= 12) { return __builtin_string_concat_inline3(self, b, c) }
        let out = str_with_capacity(total)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, self, 0, alen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen, b, 0, blen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen + blen, c, 0, clen)
        return out
    }

    public fn concat(self, b: String, c: String, d: String) -> String {
        let alen = __builtin_string_len(self)
        let blen = __builtin_string_len(b)
        let clen = __builtin_string_len(c)
        let dlen = __builtin_string_len(d)
        let total = alen + blen + clen + dlen
        if (total <= 12) { return __builtin_string_concat_inline4(self, b, c, d) }
        let out = str_with_capacity(total)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, self, 0, alen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen, b, 0, blen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen + blen, c, 0, clen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen + blen + clen, d, 0, dlen)
        return out
    }

    public fn concat(self, b: String, c: String, d: String, e: String) -> String {
        let alen = __builtin_string_len(self)
        let blen = __builtin_string_len(b)
        let clen = __builtin_string_len(c)
        let dlen = __builtin_string_len(d)
        let elen = __builtin_string_len(e)
        let total = alen + blen + clen + dlen + elen
        if (total <= 12) { return __builtin_string_concat_inline5(self, b, c, d, e) }
        let out = str_with_capacity(total)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, self, 0, alen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen, b, 0, blen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen + blen, c, 0, clen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen + blen + clen, d, 0, dlen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen + blen + clen + dlen, e, 0, elen)
        return out
    }

    public fn concat(self, b: String, c: String, d: String, e: String, f: String) -> String {
        let alen = __builtin_string_len(self)
        let blen = __builtin_string_len(b)
        let clen = __builtin_string_len(c)
        let dlen = __builtin_string_len(d)
        let elen = __builtin_string_len(e)
        let flen = __builtin_string_len(f)
        let total = alen + blen + clen + dlen + elen + flen
        if (total <= 12) { return __builtin_string_concat_inline6(self, b, c, d, e, f) }
        let out = str_with_capacity(total)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, 0, self, 0, alen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen, b, 0, blen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen + blen, c, 0, clen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen + blen + clen, d, 0, dlen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen + blen + clen + dlen, e, 0, elen)
        strCopyRangeInto(out, alen + blen + clen + dlen + elen, f, 0, flen)
        return out
    }

    // `[start, end)`. The half-open twin of `substring`, which takes a *length*.
    // Both exist because `s[a..b]` is end-exclusive and reading a range operator
    // as a length is the mistake this pair is meant to stop.
    public fn slice(self, start: Int, end: Int) -> String {
        let slen = __builtin_string_len(self)
        let mut first = start
        if (first < 0) { first = 0 }
        if (first > slen) { first = slen }
        let mut stop = end
        if (stop > slen) { stop = slen }
        if (stop < first) { stop = first }
        let count = stop - first
        // The same two cuts as `strSubstring`; see the notes there.
        if (count <= 12) { return __builtin_string_inline(self, first, count) }
        return __builtin_string_view(self, first, count)
    }

    // The byte at `index`, or NUL when out of range. Out-of-range is not an
    // error because the range check the caller would write is the one this
    // already does; NUL is distinguishable because a Prismio string cannot
    // contain one. `self.get(index)` is the Option form.
    public fn charAt(self, index: Int) -> Char {
        if (index < 0 or index >= __builtin_string_len(self)) { return '\0' }
        return __builtin_string_byte_at(self, index)
    }

    // Negative, zero or positive: by unsigned byte, then by length. Only the
    // sign is the contract; the answer is -1, 0 or 1.
    //
    // The builtin answers two short strings from their pairs alone, as one
    // byte-swapped integer compare in registers, and anything else with
    // `memcmp`. Over the `sort_strings` keys it costs 1.13 ns a call, against
    // 3.28 ns for the byte loop it replaced and 1.53 ns for libc++'s
    // `std::string::compare`. See `ir_str_compare`.
    public fn compare(self, other: String) -> Int {
        return __builtin_string_compare(self, other)
    }

    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The rest, which delegate
    // -----------------------------------------------------------------------
    public prop length(self) -> Int { return strLength(self) }
    public prop isEmpty(self) -> Bool { return strIsEmpty(self) }
    public prop isNotEmpty(self) -> Bool { return strIsEmpty(self) == false }
    public prop isBlank(self) -> Bool { return strIsBlank(self) }
    public fn clone(self) -> String { return strClone(self) }

    public fn byteAt(self, index: Int) -> Int { return strByteAt(self, index) }
    public fn get(self, index: Int) -> Option<Char> { return strGet(self, index) }
    public prop first(self) -> Char { return strFirst(self) }
    public prop last(self) -> Char { return strLast(self) }

    public fn substring(self, start: Int, length: Int) -> String {
        return strSubstring(self, start, length)
    }

    // The body lives here rather than in a prefixed free function: `equals` is
    // the public spelling, and `==` reaches the same answer.
    //
    // **This was a byte loop, and the reason it was one is gone.** The note here
    // said the builtin could not be used because the committed seed did not know
    // `__builtin_string_eq` and std has to compile under the seed -- true when it
    // was written, and no longer true since the seed was refreshed for
    // `__builtin_string_inline`. It also said the loop compared lengths first
    // "which the builtin's strcmp cannot do"; the lowering compares lengths out
    // of the pair now, and for two short strings answers from the pairs alone
    // without touching memory at all.
    public fn equals(self, other: String) -> Bool {
        return __builtin_string_eq(self, other)
    }
    public fn equalsIgnoreCase(self, other: String) -> Bool {
        return strEqualsIgnoreCase(self, other)
    }

    public fn repeat(self, times: Int) -> String { return strRepeat(self, times) }

    public fn contains(self, needle: String) -> Bool { return strContains(self, needle) }
    public fn contains(self, needle: Char) -> Bool { return strContainsChar(self, needle) }
    public fn startsWith(self, prefix: String) -> Bool { return strStartsWith(self, prefix) }
    public fn startsWith(self, prefix: Char) -> Bool { return strStartsWithChar(self, prefix) }
    public fn endsWith(self, suffix: String) -> Bool { return strEndsWith(self, suffix) }
    public fn endsWith(self, suffix: Char) -> Bool { return strEndsWithChar(self, suffix) }
    public fn matchesAt(self, needle: String, at: Int) -> Bool {
        return strMatchesAt(self, needle, at)
    }

    public fn indexOf(self, needle: String) -> Int { return strIndexOf(self, needle) }
    public fn indexOf(self, needle: Char) -> Int { return strIndexOfChar(self, needle) }
    public fn indexOf(self, needle: String, from: Int) -> Int {
        return strIndexOfFrom(self, needle, from)
    }
    public fn indexOf(self, needle: Char, from: Int) -> Int {
        return strIndexOfCharFrom(self, needle, from)
    }
    public fn lastIndexOf(self, needle: String) -> Int { return strLastIndexOf(self, needle) }
    public fn lastIndexOf(self, needle: Char) -> Int { return strLastIndexOfChar(self, needle) }
    public fn find(self, needle: String) -> Option<Int> { return strFind(self, needle) }
    public fn find(self, needle: String, from: Int) -> Option<Int> {
        return strFindFrom(self, needle, from)
    }
    public fn count(self, needle: String) -> Int { return strCountOf(self, needle) }
    public fn count(self, needle: Char) -> Int { return strCountOfChar(self, needle) }
    public fn countOf(self, needle: String) -> Int { return strCountOf(self, needle) }

    public fn trim(self) -> String { return strTrim(self) }
    public fn trimStart(self) -> String { return strTrimStart(self) }
    public fn trimEnd(self) -> String { return strTrimEnd(self) }
    public fn trimChars(self, set: String) -> String { return strTrimChars(self, set) }
    public fn padStart(self, width: Int, pad: Char) -> String { return strPadStart(self, width, pad) }
    public fn padEnd(self, width: Int, pad: Char) -> String { return strPadEnd(self, width, pad) }
    public fn padCenter(self, width: Int, pad: Char) -> String {
        return strPadCenter(self, width, pad)
    }

    public fn toUpper(self) -> String { return strToUpper(self) }
    public fn toLower(self) -> String { return strToLower(self) }
    public fn capitalize(self) -> String { return strCapitalize(self) }
    public fn reverse(self) -> String { return strReverse(self) }

    public fn replace(self, old: String, new: String) -> String {
        return strReplaceAll(self, old, new)
    }
    public fn replaceFirst(self, old: String, new: String) -> String {
        return strReplaceFirst(self, old, new)
    }
    public fn replaceAll(self, old: String, new: String) -> String {
        return strReplaceAll(self, old, new)
    }
    public fn insert(self, at: Int, other: String) -> String { return strInsert(self, at, other) }
    public fn removeRange(self, start: Int, length: Int) -> String {
        return strRemoveRange(self, start, length)
    }
    public fn stripPrefix(self, prefix: String) -> Option<String> {
        return strStripPrefix(self, prefix)
    }
    public fn stripSuffix(self, suffix: String) -> Option<String> {
        return strStripSuffix(self, suffix)
    }

    public fn split(self, sep: Char) -> Vec<String> { return strSplit(self, sep) }
    public fn split(self, sep: String) -> Vec<String> { return strSplitOn(self, sep) }
    public fn splitOn(self, sep: String) -> Vec<String> { return strSplitOn(self, sep) }
    public fn splitWhitespace(self) -> Vec<String> { return strSplitWhitespace(self) }
    public fn lines(self) -> Vec<String> { return strLines(self) }
    public fn chars(self) -> Vec<Char> { return strChars(self) }
    public fn bytes(self) -> Vec<Int> { return strBytes(self) }

    // Generic in the closure, exactly as the free functions above are. The
    // `impl` target may not be generic (src/parse/decl.psm rejects `impl Vec<T>`),
    // but a *method* may: it is a free function whose first parameter is the
    // receiver, and nothing about that stops it having type parameters.
    public fn countIf<F>(self, keep: F) -> Int { return strCountIf(self, keep) }
    public fn indexWhere<F>(self, keep: F) -> Int { return strIndexWhere(self, keep) }
    // `allChars` and `anyChars`, not `allOf` and `anyOf`. The short names are
    // std/list.psm's, over `Vec<T>`, and both sides are generic in the closure:
    // an overload set holding `allOf<T, F>(Vec<T>, F)` and `allOf<F>(String, F)`
    // could not be resolved, and test_89_closures stopped compiling the moment
    // this file claimed the name. A method is a free function, so a method name
    // is a global name -- that is the cost the header of this block is about.
    public fn allChars<F>(self, keep: F) -> Bool { return strAllChars(self, keep) }
    public fn anyChars<F>(self, keep: F) -> Bool { return strAnyChars(self, keep) }

    // **The second reading of the same bytes.** `length`, `charAt` and
    // `substring` above are byte-indexed and stay that way; these count and cut
    // by character, which is what user text needs. See the UTF-8 section for why
    // both exist.
    public prop isAscii(self) -> Bool { return strIsAscii(self) }
    public prop isValidUtf8(self) -> Bool { return strIsValidUtf8(self) }
    public prop scalarCount(self) -> Int { return strScalarCount(self) }
    public fn scalars(self) -> Vec<Int> { return strScalars(self) }
    public fn scalarAt(self, at: Int) -> Int { return strScalarAt(self, at) }
    public fn scalarWidthAt(self, at: Int) -> Int { return strScalarWidthAt(self, at) }
    public fn isCharBoundary(self, at: Int) -> Bool { return strIsCharBoundary(self, at) }
    public fn scalarSubstring(self, start: Int, count: Int) -> String {
        return strScalarSubstring(self, start, count)
    }

    public fn parseInt(self) -> Option<Int> { return strParseInt(self) }
    public fn parseInt(self, radix: Int) -> Option<Int> { return strParseIntRadix(self, radix) }
    public fn parseIntOr(self, fallback: Int) -> Int { return strParseIntOr(self, fallback) }
    public fn parseI64(self) -> Option<I64> { return strParseI64(self) }
    public fn parseI64(self, radix: Int) -> Option<I64> { return strParseI64Radix(self, radix) }
    public fn parseU64(self) -> Option<U64> { return strParseU64(self) }
    public fn parseU64(self, radix: Int) -> Option<U64> { return strParseU64Radix(self, radix) }
    public fn parseFloat(self) -> Option<Float> { return strParseFloat(self) }
    public fn parseBool(self) -> Option<Bool> { return strParseBool(self) }
}

// A `Char` is a byte. These are the `char*` free functions above under the names
// a call site reads better with, and `code` is the `as Int` cast named -- see the
// note over `charCode` in src/common/text.psm for why writing that conversion out
// by hand was once a source of silent wrongness.
impl Char {
    public prop isDigit(self) -> Bool { return charIsDigit(self) }
    public prop isLower(self) -> Bool { return charIsLower(self) }
    public prop isUpper(self) -> Bool { return charIsUpper(self) }
    public prop isAlpha(self) -> Bool { return charIsAlpha(self) }
    public prop isAlnum(self) -> Bool { return charIsAlnum(self) }
    public prop isSpace(self) -> Bool { return charIsSpace(self) }

    public fn toUpper(self) -> Char { return charToUpper(self) }
    public fn toLower(self) -> Char { return charToLower(self) }
    public prop digitValue(self) -> Int { return charDigitValue(self) }
    public prop code(self) -> Int { return self as Int }
    public fn toString(self) -> String { return strFromChar(self) }
}

// `toString` on the numeric types, so that building text out of a number reads
// the same way whatever the number is. The overload set is by exact type, as
// every overload set here is: there is no implicit numeric promotion, so
// `count.toString()` picks the `Int` body and nothing else.
impl Int {
    public fn toString(self) -> String { return strFromInt(self) }
    public fn toString(self, radix: Int) -> String { return strFromIntRadix(self, radix) }
}

impl I64 {
    public fn toString(self) -> String { return strFromInt(self as Int) }
}

impl U64 {
    public fn toString(self) -> String { return strFromUnsigned(self) }
    public fn toString(self, radix: Int) -> String { return strFromU64Radix(self, radix) }
    public fn toHex(self) -> String { return strHex(self) }
    public fn toOctal(self) -> String { return strOctal(self) }
    public fn toBinary(self) -> String { return strBinary(self) }
}

// The type that had no conversion at all until `strFromFloat`. `toString` is the
// round-trip text; `toString(decimals)` is the fixed-point presentation form.
impl Float {
    public fn toString(self) -> String { return strFromFloat(self) }
    public fn toString(self, decimals: Int) -> String { return strFromFloatFixed(self, decimals) }
}

impl Bool {
    public fn toString(self) -> String { return strFromBool(self) }
}
